    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "immut_t",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 895,
      "width": 1420,
      "height": 28,
      "text": "⚠  THE IMMUTABLE-ONCE-APPLIED RULE  (CLAUDE.md \"Performance Rules\")",
      "originalText": "⚠  THE IMMUTABLE-ONCE-APPLIED RULE  (CLAUDE.md \"Performance Rules\")",
      "fontSize": 15,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#92400e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 400003,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 563595751,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "boundElements": [],
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "containerId": "immut",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "aN",
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "updated": 1779413896305,
      "autoResize": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "immut_body",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 930,
      "width": 1420,
      "height": 180,
      "text": "Once a migration has run anywhere (including dev), it is FROZEN. Never edit it. Why: editing drifts the ModelSnapshot.cs from\nthe history table → next migration generation produces broken output OR (worse) deploying the edited migration to a DB that already\nran the old version either no-ops or corrupts schema.\n\nDestructive changes (drop column / drop table / rename / NOT NULL on existing column) — DO NOT do in one migration:\n\n   Step A:  Deploy code that no longer reads the column.   ← deploy + wait one release cycle\n   Step B:  Wait until all in-flight requests using the old code have completed.\n   Step C:  Generate a new migration that drops the column.  Deploy.\n\nDoing all three in one migration is how production outages happen during deploys: old pods crash on the missing column\nwhile new pods are still rolling out.",
      "originalText": "Once a migration has run anywhere (including dev), it is FROZEN. Never edit it. Why: editing drifts the ModelSnapshot.cs from\nthe history table → next migration generation produces broken output OR (worse) deploying the edited migration to a DB that already\nran the old version either no-ops or corrupts schema.\n\nDestructive changes (drop column / drop table / rename / NOT NULL on existing column) — DO NOT do in one migration:\n\n   Step A:  Deploy code that no longer reads the column.   ← deploy + wait one release cycle\n   Step B:  Wait until all in-flight requests using the old code have completed.\n   Step C:  Generate a new migration that drops the column.  Deploy.\n\nDoing all three in one migration is how production outages happen during deploys: old pods crash on the missing column\nwhile new pods are still rolling out.",
      "fontSize": 11,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#374151",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
